ASH for Windows, via winget
===========================

This manifest set is NOT submission-ready
-----------------------------------------

Read this section before treating these files as a submission. They are schema-valid
against Microsoft's published 1.12.0 schemas and they are checked on every pull
request, but they would be rejected by the microsoft/winget-pkgs validation pipeline
today, for one reason:

  The MSIX these manifests install is self-signed.

winget-pkgs requires an installer signed by a certificate the validation pipeline
trusts. A self-signed MSIX installs on a machine whose administrator has imported the
certificate and nowhere else, so a submitted manifest pointing at one describes a
package most people cannot install. The pipeline checks that and says so.

What would change it is a real Authenticode certificate. packaging/msix/ is built so
that swapping one is a single secret, not a rework: the signing step reads the
certificate from a repository secret and everything else about the package is already
correct. Until that secret exists, this directory is a manifest set that can be
validated and cannot be submitted, and describing it as anything else would be wrong.

Two other fields are absent for related reasons, and a submission would need both:

  PackageFamilyName   the Identity Name joined to a 13-character hash of the publisher
                      string, which is the signing certificate's subject. A wrong value
                      matches the schema pattern exactly, which is the worst failure
                      shape available: it validates, and winget would use it to
                      correlate this package with something else. The loopback set the
                      e2e job installs does carry it (see below), because `winget
                      uninstall` and `winget upgrade --manifest` cannot find an
                      installed MSIX without it; a release set cannot until there is a
                      real certificate.
  SignatureSha256     the digest of the signature file inside the package. For a
                      self-signed build it describes a signature no machine trusts, so
                      publishing it would assert something untrue about the artifact.

Nothing in packaging/ submits to microsoft/winget-pkgs, and nothing here opens a pull
request against it. That is a separate decision nobody has made.

Why the installer is the MSIX
-----------------------------

The MSIX under packaging/msix/ is the Windows artifact this branch produces, so it is
what winget installs. The alternative considered and rejected was inventing a third
Windows artifact, a portable zip, so that winget had something else to point at. That
would mean building, testing, and maintaining an installer format for no reason other
than making one manifest easier to write, and it would leave two Windows packages whose
behavior could drift apart.

The files, and why they are named this way
-----------------------------------------

winget's multi-file manifest format is three documents describing one version of one
package, and the filenames are part of the format:

  Amazon.AutomatedSecurityHelper.yaml                 version manifest. Names the
                                                      package, the version, and which
                                                      locale is the default.
  Amazon.AutomatedSecurityHelper.installer.yaml       installer manifest. What to
                                                      download, its digest, and how it
                                                      behaves once installed.
  Amazon.AutomatedSecurityHelper.locale.en-US.yaml    default-locale manifest. Publisher,
                                                      license, description, tags.

Verified against a published set rather than inferred: manifests/a/Amazon/Kiro/1.1.14/
in microsoft/winget-pkgs holds Amazon.Kiro.yaml, Amazon.Kiro.installer.yaml,
Amazon.Kiro.locale.en-US.yaml, and Amazon.Kiro.locale.zh-CN.yaml.

The publisher namespace is Amazon, not AWSLabs. manifests/a/Amazon/ already carries 25
packages including AWSCLI, SAM-CLI, Corretto, and Kiro, and there is no reason to
invent a second namespace for the same publisher.

Which schema version, and why 1.12.0
------------------------------------

1.12.0 is the newest manifest version that exists. Three independent readings agree:
winget-pkgs documents doc/manifest/schema/1.12.0/ and has no 1.13.0 directory; all
three aka.ms schema links for 1.12.0 return real JSON whose $id names the URL that
served it, while 1.13.0 does not; and a set published to winget-pkgs on 2026-09-14 is
authored at 1.12.0.

Newest rather than merely available: an older ManifestVersion still installs, but the
winget-pkgs pipeline validates new submissions against the current schema, so an older
version would have to be raised before this set could be submitted at all. Nothing here
needs to support a client older than the schema, because nothing here is published to a
source any client reads.

One thing to know before raising it: the version numbers are not contiguous. 1.7.0 and
1.11.0 have no schemas and no documentation. And aka.ms does not 404 for a version that
does not exist. It answers HTTP 200 with a Bing search page, 67 KB of HTML, which a
validator that only checked the status code would happily treat as a schema. That is
why validate-manifests.py requires every fetched schema to parse as JSON and to carry
the $id of the URL it was fetched from.

The digest, and why it is 64 zeros
----------------------------------

InstallerSha256 is required by the schema and must be 64 hex characters, so it cannot
be blank or commented out while the artifact it describes does not exist yet. The
checked-in value is 64 zeros, and validate-manifests.py asserts that it still is.

The alternative is worse in a way that is easy to miss: a plausible-looking digest
validates. It also keeps validating after the artifact it claims to describe is rebuilt,
because nothing compares the two. A digest of all zeros cannot be mistaken for a
measurement, and the assertion that it is still the sentinel turns "somebody committed
a digest for an artifact nobody built" from an invisible state into a failed check.

set-release-metadata.py fills it, from the .msix it is handed:

  uv run packaging/winget/set-release-metadata.py \
      --msix dist/automated_security_helper-3.7.0-x64.msix \
      --out-dir build/winget

It writes a copy and never edits in place, because editing in place would delete the
sentinel the assertion depends on. Four fields come out of the artifact rather than out
of anyone's memory: InstallerSha256, InstallerUrl (built from the release tag and the
file's own name, so a filename that does not match this directory's default corrects
itself), Architecture, and MinimumOSVersion. The last two are read from the AppxManifest
inside the .msix, which is what keeps them in step with packaging/msix/ without a second
hand-maintained copy. The script then runs validate-manifests.py --released against its
own output, which requires the opposite digest state.

Three names on PATH, and why the command is ashx
------------------------------------------------

The installer manifest's Commands list records what the package puts on PATH. The
moniker is `ash` (`winget install ash`), but that is a distribution identifier; the
command is `ashx`:

  ashx                       canonical, and the one to use.
  ashv3                      deprecated. It works and warns once on stderr. The name
                             pins a major version, so it reads wrong the moment v4
                             exists.
  automated-security-helper  kept indefinitely and silent.

The wheel also declares `ash`, the v3 command, as a deprecated alias, and the MSIX
deliberately does not expose it. Windows is the reason. MSYS2 ships the Almquist shell
as ash.exe, and on a machine where that is earlier on PATH than ASH's entry point, a
bare `ash` reaches the shell and fails with `Illegal option --`. That has already
happened in this project's CI, and it is why the v4 command is `ashx`. The long name is
the escape hatch for a host where some other program claims a short name.

Every one of these three has to be an AppExecutionAlias in the MSIX manifest.
validate-manifests.py requires the Commands list to match [project.scripts] in
pyproject.toml minus the deprecated `ash` (NOT_EXPOSED_SCRIPTS), and refuses a Commands
list that names `ash`, so a new console script cannot be added without this list
noticing.

Two flags worth knowing because they are easy to get backwards: -V is --version, and -v
is --verbose.

How this is tested
------------------

  uv run packaging/winget/validate-manifests.py

Two jobs in .github/workflows/ash-package.yml run this directory. The `winget` job runs
the command above. It needs no Windows machine and does not need the wheel.

The `winget-client` job runs packaging/winget/verify-on-windows.ps1 on windows-latest
against the .msix the `msix` job built. It gets a winget client, runs `winget validate`
on the release-rendered set, requires a manifest with a wrong InstallerSha256 to be
refused, installs through `winget install --manifest`, runs the three shared e2e cases
(tests/e2e/README.md), uninstalls, installs an N-1 build, upgrades it with
`winget upgrade --manifest`, and uninstalls again. No release asset exists for an
unreleased commit, so it serves the .msix from 127.0.0.1 and renders a loopback set for
it with `set-release-metadata.py --local-url-base`. validate-manifests.py
`--local-installer` accepts only a loopback URL there, so that set cannot pass as a
release set, and requires a PackageFamilyName. set-release-metadata.py computes that
from the AppxManifest's Identity Name and Publisher, and after each install
verify-on-windows.ps1 requires it to equal the one Get-AppxPackage reports. The client must be at least
winget 1.12.210, the first release with the 1.12 manifest schemas.

The runner image documentation for windows-latest lists Chocolatey and does not mention
winget. The script therefore uses a client already registered for the runner account if
there is one, then tries to register the App Installer that Windows Server 2025 can
carry, then installs a pinned winget-cli release whose downloads are checked against
SHA-256 digests in the script. If none of the three yields a working client, the job
fails. It does not skip.

The schemas are still checked on their own. They are a real check, because they carry every required
field, pattern, and enum the client enforces. The script also checks the four things a
schema cannot: that the three files agree with each other, that the version they declare
is the version this repository builds, that the installer URL names the matching release
tag, and that Commands is exactly the console scripts pyproject.toml declares, minus
the deprecated `ash`.

Before it validates anything it corrupts one field per manifest in memory and requires
each copy to be rejected, and requires the rejection message to name the field that was
corrupted. That second half matters: a locale manifest with a deliberately invalid
PackageLocale was, during development, rejected for a missing Publisher instead, and the
control read as passing while proving nothing about the constraint it was written to
exercise.
